{"body":{"text":["The point of putting everything in one read was still that we would dissect it, so that components don't re-render. But of course, any type of latency is a problem with something that is so real-time as typing. So having everything in one read, making the read longer in the engine is, of course, also contributing to that. So the question is if separate reads and subscriptions then would make that smaller, then of course that's helpful. Regarding checking every store, I think we have discerned that with the right structure, we'll be able to, through commits, track where something exists. In order for something to exist in another store, it must be shipped with the information of where it came from. So with that, all should be trackable. And with that, we ought to be able to have a different way of looking at things. Is that what we tried to do before? Not as I recall, but I might be wrong. Good that we agree on IO draw. If we look at the example of the collations and the reader, it is probably the reader that requires everything below it to be wrapped or possibly wrapped. Either everything's wrapped, but the wrapper doesn't do any visual thing, or you toggle the wrapping. But then if you change a parent, I don't know what that means for DOM trees, because then it's really that it's kind of like offers, right? We're stating here to use this wrapping thing, and then that wrapping thing can take the offer, or the other way around, the wrapping thing then, or maybe it should come through the offer. I don't know. Again, this is complicated things to think about for me. So for ownership and the overlay, are we talking about the holding reference is actually of the input, but then it's only a pointing reference in the overlay? Is that it? That means that we have essentially portals, right? You can point to anything and then you render it in that place as well. Well, you're talking about functions that take in a value, but what's the point of pressing a function? You still have to handle the output. So I don't know quite what you mean there. Programs is another thing. They dispatch side effects. But why don't you tell me the actual actions that we consider that we have today? Then we can be more practical and grounded in our reasoning, because I don't feel that I am. On 1.7, it just sounds like the chassis doesn't behave properly. I mean, whenever you draw something, then it's now its own in the hierarchy. So of course, propose should work with that. But propose to me, always replacing what you were given, might not be right. Now we don't have any kind of permission boundaries yet, but you also be able to propose all kinds of things that you have been given permission to once there's permissions. So having proposed being tightly coupled to what you're given might not be the right thing. But naturally, if I draw a component through code, then of course that component ought to have its propose be relative, if that is the way that the propose is supposed to work. Or maybe propose always takes a more appropriate commit, you know. So the way of writing commits become more first class, not automagical like propose."]},"id":"1M8926W1ZMWG000","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
